# Lower-question design principle

- Do not default to "one row per group with several aggregates" for every new question.
- Aggregation questions are still encouraged, but they should be part of the mix rather than the dominant default format.
- Rotate prompt formats across questions, for example:
  - row-level filtering/classification tasks
  - event timeline / first-occurrence logic
  - debugging/fix-the-query tasks
  - data quality checks and exception finding
  - joins with inclusion/exclusion logic
  - light window-function tasks when level-appropriate
- Rotate business domains/data marts (support, logistics, finance, subscriptions, growth, operations) so consecutive questions are not near-duplicates in shape.
- Keep lower-difficulty scope small and routine, but vary the answer shape (not always grouped summaries).
- When adding a new question, briefly sanity-check against recent interview-style patterns for inspiration and avoid cloning the previous question structure.

# Grading principle (all levels)

- Across lower/core/higher questions, prioritize logical correctness over presentation details by default.
- Treat ordering, alias names, and output formatting as non-blocking unless the prompt explicitly says they are required for correctness.
- Do not reject an otherwise-correct solution only because sort order or cosmetic naming differs from the reference.
